\details{
\strong{Reading the diagnostic views.} A percentile CI comprises two quantiles of
the bootstrap distribution, and quantiles are uninformative about shape. The
two diagnostic types exist to check the assumptions that make those two
numbers meaningful.

\code{type = "histogram"} shows each parameter's marginal draw distribution.
Three features matter:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Bimodality}: the optimiser is settling into two distinct basins
depending on which recordings a resample happens to contain. A percentile
CI spanning both modes describes an interval the parameter is rarely
actually in.
\item \strong{A spike at a bound}: \code{\link[stats:nls]{stats::nls()}} is called with \code{algorithm = "port"}
under box constraints, which are absorbing. A resample driven onto a bound
stays there. A visible point mass at, say, exactly \code{0.30} means the
reported CI endpoint is a property of the constraint rather than of the
data.
\item \strong{Heavy skew}: the regime in which the percentile interval is least
accurate and a bias-corrected-and-accelerated (BCa) interval would be
preferable.
}

\code{type = "pairs"} shows the joint distribution. A tight diagonal ridge
between two parameters means the data do not identify them separately,so
their marginal intervals understate the joint uncertainty, and neither
parameter should be interpreted while holding the other fixed.

These views describe the \emph{fitting procedure's} stability under resampling.
A clean unimodal, uncorrelated set of draws means the bootstrap behaved, not
that the curve model is necessarily the right description of the data.
}
